Instruction: Read the segment of an acquisition agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Acquisition Agreement: SECTION 8.2. Comprehensive Evaluation. 
(a) Notwithstanding any other provision herein, upon receipt of any Acquisition Proposal, the Board is required to conduct a holistic review within a three Business Day notice period, taking into account financial sustainability, strategic alignment, and operational feasibility concurrently; 
(b) The determination of a Superior Proposal shall be based on a dual assessment whereby the Proposal must not only meet rigorous financial benchmarks—as validated by consultation with designated financial advisors—but also demonstrate clear strategic value and operational viability, thereby precluding reliance on any single evaluative metric such as the financial point of view alone; 
(c) In events where Parent submits subsequent revisions to the Proposal, the Board is obliged to re-assess all material aspects in light of the revised terms, ensuring that the decision-making process integrates both monetary and non-monetary criteria without exclusive deference to financial analyses. (Pages 89-90)
Choices:
